<v Sam Gebler>Seriously, right? I mean, that was like-- we've had plenty of surf rescues and cliff rescues before, but this COVID thing is new. And it's the difficulty of calls and the stress of calls is much higher than normal. I mean, you could have had 30 calls a year ago. And it would be less stressful than having 15 calls in this new COVID era.
<v Sam Gebler>真的，对吧？我的意思是，那就像——我们以前有过很多次冲浪救援和悬崖救援，但这个新冠事情是新的。呼叫的难度和压力比平时高得多。我的意思是，一年前你可能会有30次呼叫。而那会比在这个新冠新时代有15次呼叫压力小。

00:49:11.490 --> 00:49:34.680
<v Dana Chivvis>The kids were OK, by the way, and Sam didn't get COVID. But for so many of us, the specter of COVID is always peering over our shoulders, intensifying everything we do, from the most extreme activities-- the surf rescue, a cliff rescue-- to the most banal-- grocery shopping, a teeth cleaning. That anxiety is the ether we exist in now.
<v Dana Chivvis>顺便说一句，孩子们没事，萨姆也没得新冠。但对我们许多人来说，新冠的幽灵总是徘徊在我们的肩膀之上，强化我们所做的一切，从最极端的活动——冲浪救援、悬崖救援——到最平庸的——买杂货、洗牙。那种焦虑是我们现在生存的以太。

00:49:34.680 --> 00:49:58.160
<v Dana Chivvis>There is one thing that's given Sam some comfort recently-- the vaccine. A few weeks ago, he had his second dose. He said it lifted a lot of stress for him. Now he only has to deal with fires, sharks, cliffs, people who've stopped breathing-- you know, regular work stuff.
