<v Chris Gethard>--you don't have to. You don't have that many guilts over that. I mean, I was so good at hiding it, so good at hiding it. And I don't know why. I don't know why I never let you and mom know about that. It's just not my instinct. My instinct is that that's going to worry you guys so much, and you're going to have to sit around and then be scared about me, and I don't want you doing that. And I always figured maybe I could push through it, or it would pass. Or even I felt like maybe I'm just a moody kid.
<v Chris Gethard>——你不必那样。你没那么多内疚感。我是说，我太擅长隐藏它了，太擅长隐藏它了。我不知道为什么。我不知道为什么我从没让你们了解。那不是我的直觉。我的直觉是这会让你非常担心，你们得坐下来担心我，我不想你们那样。我一直想也许我能挺过去，或者它会过去。甚至我觉得也许我只是个喜怒无常的孩子。

00:54:55.470 --> 00:55:19.980
<v Ken Gethard>Yeah, if you're feeling bad, you need help and stuff like that, Chris, I would like you to come to us at any point in time. But I'm not a professional. I don't know what to tell you. I don't know if I'm making sense. I want to be there, but I don't want to tell you something wrong either. That's always in the back of my head. It's like, oh God, if he comes to me-- you know.
<v Ken Gethard>是的，如果你感觉不好，你需要帮助，诸如此类，克里斯，我希望你在任何时候来找我们。但我不是专业人士。我不知道该告诉你什么。我不知道我是否说得对。我想在那里，但我也不想告诉你错误的东西。这总是在我的脑后。就像，噢，天哪，如果他来找我——你知道。

00:55:19.980 --> 00:55:22.400
<v Ken Gethard>[LAUGHTER]
<v Ken Gethard>[笑声]

00:55:22.400 --> 00:55:26.870
<v Ken Gethard>Am I going to tell him something, and all of a sudden, he's going to be worse?
<v Ken Gethard>我是不是要告诉他什么，然后突然间，他变得更糟了？

00:55:26.870 --> 00:55:28.010
<v Chris Gethard>Yeah.
<v Chris Gethard>是的。

00:55:28.010 --> 00:55:47.630
<v Ken Gethard>I'm scared. I don't think I want you coming to me-- dad, I'm upset. I need you to talk me. I wouldn't know what to say. That's what you have a professional for, you know? And it's-- does that make sense anywhere there?
<v Ken Gethard>我很害怕。我不觉得我想要你来找我——爸，我很沮丧。我需要你跟我说话。我不知道该说什么。那是专业人士做的事，你知道吗？这——哪一点有道理吗？

00:55:47.630 --> 00:56:14.710
<v Chris Gethard>Yeah. No, because I know that. I know that's the truth. I know that's the truth. I know you're not the guy to talk about that. It's part of what made it very hard to say it. But it doesn't-- I think I let that fear make me not talk to you about it. Whereas what really happened is exactly what I should have known you would have done, which was you're not the guy to talk about it, but you're certainly going to make sure that I find the person--
<v Chris Gethard>是的。不，因为我知道。我知道那是事实。我知道那是事实。我知道你不是谈论那个的人。这部分使得很难说出口。但这并不——我觉得我让那个恐惧使我没和你们谈。而真正发生的是我早就应该知道你们会做的，也就是你不是谈论那个的人，但你绝对会确保我找到那个人——

00:56:14.710 --> 00:56:15.810
<v Ken Gethard>I'm going to get you to the right person, yeah.
<v Ken Gethard>我会把你送到正确的人那儿，是的。

00:56:15.810 --> 00:56:38.460
<v Chris Gethard>You're going to get me there. You're going to-- I mean, that's one of the things I regret the most, is like, you're not the guy-- you were never going to be the guy who I could come to and say, dad, I'm really sad, and I don't know why. You weren't going to know what to say, but I should have given a lot more credit to knowing you were going to run through a wall to get me where I needed to go.
<v Chris Gethard>你会把我送去那儿。你会——我是说，我最后悔的一件事是，你不是那个人——你永远不会是那个我可以来找他说，爸，我很伤心，但我不知道为什么的人。你不会知道该说什么，但我应该给予更多信任，我知道你会冲破墙壁把我送到我需要去的地方。

00:56:38.460 --> 00:56:52.080
<v Ken Gethard>Yeah.
<v Ken Gethard>是的。

00:56:52.080 --> 00:57:12.360
<v Ira Glass>Chris Gethard and his dad, Ken Gethard. Thanks to them for agreeing to have this conversation on tape. The story first ran on our show a few years back. Chris is the host of the long running podcast, Beautiful Anonymous.
<v Ira Glass>克里斯·盖萨德和他父亲肯·盖萨德。感谢他们同意录下这次谈话。这个故事几年前首次在我们的节目播出。克里斯是长青播客《美丽的匿名者》的主持人。

00:57:12.360 --> 00:57:40.060
